    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nBillionaire Rocket Pioneer Sir Peter Beck Becomes the Proud Owner of a Jetson One\n8 photos\nPhoto: Jetson Aero\nWith its entire 2026 and 2027 production sold out a while ago, it’s crystal clear that everyone wants a Jetson One, but the fact that some of these owners are amazing tech innovators on their own proves that this contraption is far more than a trendy personal aircraft. The Jetson One is making history, and even Rocket Lab CEO Peter Beck agrees.\nA year ago, the maker of the Jetson One personal eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing) made headlines with the announcement of the first official owner of the Limited Edition Jetson One. That man was 33-year-old Palmer Luckey, founder of the defense tech startup Anduril Industries.\nLuckey was one of the first to order a Jetson personal aircraft, and to trust in this groundbreaking technology. He was also willing to wait patiently for the delivery, as someone who understands the inevitable challenges that any tech startup faces in the beginning. He was supposed to get his Jetson One in 2023, but that ended up happening in September, 2025.\nJetson’s CTO Tomasz Patan and CEO Stephan D’haene flew to Carlsbad, California, to guide Luckey on his first Jetson One flight – although he turned out to be a natural who needed minimal training.\nNow, an even more famous owner is making Jetson’s CTO and CEO proud. It’s none other than Sir Peter Beck himself, founder and CEO of Rocket Lab. What started as a small company is now the third most frequent orbital launcher in the world, after SpaceX and the Chinese government, after moving its headquarters to California more than a decade ago.\nThe Electron rocket, widely considered one of the most successful launch vehicles of all time, turned Sir Peter Beck into a rocket pioneer.\nThe New Zealand native who used to build rockets in his free time is currently a billionaire and a knight (he was appointed a Knight Companion of the New Zealand Order of Merit in 2024, becoming Sir Peter Beck). And, while Rocket Lab is working on what is claimed to be the world’s largest carbon composite rocket, Neutron, Sir Peter is getting ready for some personal fun aboard his Jetson One.\nAccording to Jetson, Sir Peter’s Jetson One (a Founders Edition unit) was already delivered and he could take to the sky by Christmas, as soon as regulatory approvals kick into place.\nFor the Jetson team, this comes as a powerful confirmation and tribute. Before ordering his own, Sir Peter flew the Jetson One during a California visit and was immediately convinced. He applauded the Jetson engineers for making complex vertical electric flight “effortless and intuitive”, also speaking of the “sheer joy of flight” he experienced with the Jetson One.\nThe Jetson One is currently priced at $148,000 (without taxes) and available for order, with earlier deliveries set for 2028.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nJetson One personal eVTOL personal aircraft Jetson Aero Peter Beck electric aircraft evtol\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT The New R36 Nissan GT-R Is Finally Here, Albeit Only in Spirit – Rendering",
